---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Choosing Safe AI Agent Autonomy Levels · San Lee"
  description="A measured framework raises AI-agent autonomy on a classifier. I defined the four levels in advance. The evidence supports two levels and rejects two."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/autonomy-ladder.html"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Ladder-verdict plate (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: four rungs,
       two shipped, two taken back. The ol.ladder below carries the full
       telling; this plate is the scoreboard.

       The sizing rule that sat here is DELETED, not out-cascaded. The
       MONOGRAPH figure ladder owns the plate scale now (ADR-014). The plate
       sits inside a .fig--plate wrapper, and `.fig--plate > svg` in
       public/assets/style.css caps it at --fig-plate. The old cap was 260px
       against a 260-unit viewBox. That cap held scale 1.000 at every width.
       The ladder breaks that discipline on purpose, so this page owes a
       measurement. The .rp-id note below carries it. */
    .rung-plate .rp-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    /* 9 units, not 8: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5).
       Measured with the pinned Playwright at a 320px viewport, the plate
       renders 272px against a 260-unit viewBox, a scale of 1.046. The old 8
       units rendered 8.00px at the old 1.000 scale. At 1.046 the same 8 units
       give 8.37px. Both sit under the floor. 9 units render 9.42px, measured.
       The step-up raises the declared units at the plate's own scale, which
       is the remedy the floor names. The text content does not move. */
    .rung-plate .rp-id {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .rung-plate .rp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* The shipped cell is a knockout box: --bg for the fill, so it reads as
       bare ground. Inside a .fig the night theme scopes --bg to --fig-ground
       (ADR-014, the READOUT graft). So the knockout now tracks the lifted
       figure ground instead of punching a darker hole.

       The stroke moves from --text to --stroke-figure, the figure layer's
       strong rank (ADR-014, section 4). That holds PL-10 inside the plate:
       the cell's own label stays ink, and the box that carries it steps down
       one rank. The retired and declined cells below keep --accent-warm,
       because a warm stroke is a verdict and not a frame. */
    .rung-plate .rp-ok {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .rung-plate .rp-ok-text {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .rung-plate .rp-cut {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .rung-plate .rp-cut-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    /* The plate's own caption. It is a <p> and not a <figcaption>. A bare
       plate wraps in <div class="fig fig--plate"> and never in <figure>,
       because the figure gate reads a <figure> as a two-slot figure. So this
       rule restates the consolidated caption metrics rather than inheriting
       them. The origin is the block headed "The caption grammar, one home" in
       public/assets/style.css (ADR-014). It carries the note voice, because
       this line argues rather than concedes. */
    .rung-plate-caption {
      max-width: var(--content);
      margin: 14px auto 0;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      line-height: 1.55;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }

    /* The two-slot caption block that sat here is DELETED. Its origin comment
       named OC-F of the redesign brief as the consolidation, and OC-F closed.
       public/assets/style.css now carries .fig-what, .fig-limit, and the
       shared figcaption metrics in one home (ADR-014).

       The lane N caption-link rule goes with it. Its whole job was to keep a
       caption link off the browser default #0000ee. That default measures
       1.97:1 in the dark theme. The consolidated `figcaption a` rule carries
       the accent colour now. I read that rule in the live stylesheet before
       the deletion. I did not assume it. */

    /* al-s1. The three L2 experiments, re-laid as a strip (ADR-013).

       A STRIP and not a plate, for two reasons the brief states. The strip
       re-lays text nodes that already sit on the page, so it cannot break
       claim parity. And the text stays HTML, so contrast-check.cjs still reads
       it. No gate reads SVG text.

       The strip carries no number and no hue. Every number sits in .fig-what,
       where a reader checks it against the two linked decision records. The
       reversal state already has two carriers on this page: the .rung-plate
       L2 cell, and the ol.ladder rung marker. A third statement of one fact is
       the visual doing prose's job badly, so the strip states none. The result
       column is the emphasis, because the result is what each row argues.

       Rows below 48rem, two columns above it. The house grammar is ix-s1 on
       index.astro: the claim, then the thing it resolves to. The content track
       is 34rem, so a three-across grid would break to 2 + 1 and read as a
       ragged table. Nothing here is a link, so scripts/hit-target.cjs has no
       standalone anchor to measure. */
    .l2-strip {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 16px 0 0;
    }
    .l2-strip li {
      min-width: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      padding: 11px 0;
    }
    .l2-strip li:first-child {
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    }
    .l2-way {
      display: block;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    .l2-verdict {
      display: block;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-top: 4px;
    }
    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
      .l2-strip li {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 14rem;
        gap: 4px 24px;
        align-items: start;
      }
      .l2-verdict {
        margin-top: 0;
      }
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="defense-news-classifier.html">&larr; Back to the classifier</a>
    <h1>The Autonomy Ladder</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">The same classifier, more self-direction one level at a time.</p>

    <p>
      Each level is the same classifier with more self-direction. The axis is
      who drives. A reach for a tool does not move a level. A decision the
      system makes for itself does.
    </p>

    <!-- Four rungs, and I took two back. The word and the stroke come first.
         Oxide marks only the rungs that I retired or declined. ADR-010 does
         not apply.

         The plate sits in a <div class="fig fig--plate"> and never in a
         <figure>. The figure gate reads a <figure> as a two-slot figure, and
         this plate carries one caption line. data-place="spine" pins the
         wrapper to the content track. That is the MONOGRAPH per-kind default
         for a plate (ADR-014).

         It takes Plate 01, the first number in the page's figure sequence.
         The backward-edge plate inside the L4 rung takes no number. It sits
         inside ol.ladder, which ADR-004 Decision 5 protects. It is rung
         chrome, not a figure of the page.

         The <svg> below keeps its own indentation. The plate is byte-exact
         and its four rung names are baseline-frozen, so the wrapper adds a
         level of nesting and re-indents nothing. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
    <svg class="rung-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 132" role="img"
         aria-label="Autonomy ladder scoreboard. L1 single call: shipped. L2 augmented: retired. L3 autonomous loop: shipped. L4 multi-agent: declined.">
      <title>L1 shipped, L2 retired, L3 shipped, L4 declined.</title>
      <text class="rp-lane" x="4" y="12">THE CLIMB</text>

      <text class="rp-id" x="4" y="28">L1</text>
      <rect class="rp-ok" x="4" y="34" width="122" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="rp-title" x="65" y="48" text-anchor="middle">single call</text>
      <text class="rp-ok-text" x="65" y="62" text-anchor="middle">SHIPPED</text>

      <text class="rp-id" x="134" y="28">L2</text>
      <rect class="rp-cut" x="134" y="34" width="122" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="rp-title" x="195" y="48" text-anchor="middle">augmented</text>
      <text class="rp-cut-text" x="195" y="62" text-anchor="middle">RETIRED</text>

      <text class="rp-id" x="4" y="84">L3</text>
      <rect class="rp-ok" x="4" y="90" width="122" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="rp-title" x="65" y="104" text-anchor="middle">loop</text>
      <text class="rp-ok-text" x="65" y="118" text-anchor="middle">SHIPPED</text>

      <text class="rp-id" x="134" y="84">L4</text>
      <rect class="rp-cut" x="134" y="90" width="122" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="rp-title" x="195" y="104" text-anchor="middle">multi-agent</text>
      <text class="rp-cut-text" x="195" y="118" text-anchor="middle">DECLINED</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="rung-plate-caption">
      The measurements sit in the rungs below.
    </p>
    </div>
    <!-- The ladder is a climb, not a table. It carries the same payload the
         four-row table carried: what it gains, who drives, and the state. A
         table cannot show two of those things. L2 has a climbed-then-retired
         arc, and L4 has a backward critic edge. The layout is vertical by
         design. It is a ladder, and vertical is what survives 320px. -->
    <p class="flow-line">
      L1 a human runs it <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span> L2 + a tool
      <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span> L3 it decides it is done
      <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span> L4 agents coordinate
    </p>

    <ol class="ladder">
      <li class="rung" data-state="shipped">
        <p class="rung-head">
          <span class="rung-id">L1</span>
          <span class="rung-name">Single call</span>
          <span class="rung-state">Shipped &middot; v1</span>
        </p>
        <p class="rung-gain">Prompt, structured output, the eval harness.</p>
        <p class="rung-driver"><span class="lbl">Who drives</span> A human runs each item.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="rung" data-state="retired">
        <p class="rung-head">
          <span class="rung-id">L2</span>
          <span class="rung-name">Augmented</span>
          <span class="rung-state">Shipped v2.0.0, then retired</span>
        </p>
        <p class="rung-gain">BM25 retrieval grounds the label in a corpus.</p>
        <p class="rung-driver"><span class="lbl">Who drives</span> A human runs it; the model reaches for a tool.</p>
        <!-- No explanatory note here on purpose. The struck marker and the
             "then retired" chip carry the climb-down; the paragraph below the
             ladder already carries the reasoning, and the measured numbers live
             in "The grounding that did not pay" above. Saying it a third time
             would be the visual doing prose's job badly. -->
      </li>

      <li class="rung" data-state="shipped">
        <p class="rung-head">
          <span class="rung-id">L3</span>
          <span class="rung-name">Autonomous loop</span>
          <span class="rung-state">Climbed &middot; both rungs</span>
        </p>
        <p class="rung-gain">Wrapped in a loop that iterates to an explicit done-signal.</p>
        <p class="rung-driver"><span class="lbl">Who drives</span> The system decides when it&rsquo;s done.</p>
        <!-- One level, two sub-rungs; both are now climbed, and each one's
             honest headline is the held-out set disagreeing with the loop. -->
        <ul class="subrungs">
          <li data-done="yes">rung 1 &mdash; prompt-optimization loop <span>shipped, rode the v2.1.0 tag</span></li>
          <li data-done="yes">rung 2 &mdash; agent-driven ML loop <span>built &middot; its live run gamed its own metric, and got caught</span></li>
        </ul>
      </li>

      <li class="rung" data-state="retired">
        <p class="rung-head">
          <span class="rung-id">L4</span>
          <span class="rung-name">Multi-agent</span>
          <span class="rung-state">Built, measured &middot; declined</span>
        </p>
        <p class="rung-gain">Decomposed: triage, classify, and a critic that can hand work <em>backward</em>.</p>
        <p class="rung-driver"><span class="lbl">Who drives</span> Multiple agents coordinate.</p>
        <!-- L4's honesty test drawn rather than described: the backward edge is
             what separates a critic from a linear pipeline with extra steps. -->
        <svg class="backedge" viewBox="0 0 260 104" role="img"
             aria-label="Triage passes to classify, classify passes to critic, and the critic hands work backward to triage.">
          <defs>
            <marker id="be-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                    markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
              <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
            </marker>
            <!-- Separate marker so the backward edge's head picks up the accent
                 with the line; markers don't inherit the referencing path's stroke. -->
            <marker id="be-arrow-back" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                    markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
              <path class="be-head-back" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <!-- rx matches --radius-sm (2px). These are viewBox units, and max-width
               caps the SVG at its intrinsic 260, so they are CSS pixels 1:1 from
               390 up; at 320 the same ~0.93 scale the be-note comment records
               paints them at 1.86px. Measured, not assumed. -->
          <rect class="be-box" x="4" y="14" width="72" height="34" rx="2" />
          <text class="be-label" x="40" y="36" text-anchor="middle">Triage</text>
          <rect class="be-box" x="86" y="14" width="72" height="34" rx="2" />
          <text class="be-label" x="122" y="36" text-anchor="middle">Classify</text>
          <rect class="be-box" x="168" y="14" width="72" height="34" rx="2" />
          <text class="be-label" x="204" y="36" text-anchor="middle">Critic</text>
          <path class="be-edge" d="M 76 31 L 84 31" marker-end="url(#be-arrow)" />
          <path class="be-edge" d="M 158 31 L 166 31" marker-end="url(#be-arrow)" />
          <!-- The one edge that matters: critic back to triage. -->
          <path class="be-edge be-back" d="M 204 48 C 204 84, 40 84, 40 50"
                marker-end="url(#be-arrow-back)" />
          <text class="be-note" x="122" y="99" text-anchor="middle">hands work backward</text>
        </svg>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      L2 asks one question. Does something to lean on help the model? I asked
      it three ways.
    </p>
    <!-- al-s1. Three ways in, three verdicts out. The strip re-lays the words
         the paragraph already used, and every number moves into .fig-what.
         The L2 rung above carries no explanatory note on purpose. This strip
         does not put one there either. The struck marker and the "then
         retired" chip already carry the climb-down.

         Strip 02 in the page's figure sequence. data-place="spine" pins it to
         the content track, the MONOGRAPH per-kind default for a strip
         (ADR-014).

         The strip is not a reversal figure. It reports three verdicts, and
         one of them is a retirement. The doubled oxide tick would state that
         retirement a third time, after the plate and the L2 rung. -->
    <figure class="fig al-s1" data-fig="strip" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Strip 02</span>
      <ul class="l2-strip" aria-label="Three L2 experiments and the verdict each one returned">
        <li>
          <span class="l2-way">Retrieved neighbor documents as context</span>
          <span class="l2-verdict">Harmful</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="l2-way">Machine-mined keyword features</span>
          <span class="l2-verdict">Helpful on the set they were mined from, harmful off it</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="l2-way">Retrieved <em>labeled</em> examples as few-shot exemplars</span>
          <span class="l2-verdict">Inert</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">One L2 question, asked three ways, returned
        three different verdicts. The neighbor documents fixed 0 domain calls
        and broke 4
        (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/archive/012-retire-bm25-grounding.md">ADR-012</a>).
        The few-shot exemplars scored 91.0% against a 90.0% baseline on an
        n=300 paired test, and p=0.70
        (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/archive/019-knn-exemplar-fewshot.md">ADR-019</a>).
        L3 below measures the keyword features.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">The strip holds no number; every number sits
        in the caption above and in the two linked records. It does not show
        the keyword-feature measurement. L3 below carries it.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      L2 stays on the record as climbed, then retired.
    </p>
    <p>
      L3&rsquo;s two rungs each have a recorded run on the
      <a href="loop-replay.html">run replay</a>. Rung 1 is the
      prompt-optimization loop. It gained on the set it tuned against, and it
      slipped on held-out gold. Rung 2 is an agent that does error-driven
      feature engineering on a trained baseline
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/archive/018-agent-driven-ml-loop.md">ADR-018</a>).
      It scored +6.0 on the validation split it could see, and
      <strong>&minus;8.6 on the human-labeled set it could not</strong>. The
      loop&rsquo;s design gave the held-out set veto power, and it used it.
    </p>
    <p>
      L4&rsquo;s honesty test is the backward edge. I aimed the critic at the
      one named, rule-checkable error cluster. That cluster is seven rows. In
      each row the model inferred a region from the US actor, and the text
      stated no place. The critic fixed <strong>six of the seven</strong>. The
      honesty test passed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pipeline still lost
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/archive/020-l4-multi-agent-pipeline.md">ADR-020</a>).
      The critic&rsquo;s restraint lived only in its prompt. It challenged
      4&times; more often than its charter allowed. Outside its target it
      broke more labels than it fixed. That is the repo&rsquo;s first
      <em>statistically significant</em> harm: domain 91.3% &rarr; 86.7% on
      the n=300 paired test, p=0.016, at 4&times; the cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both halves are the finding. The backward edge works. An agent whose
      scope rests on prose instead of code will spend that power freely.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/006-autonomy-ladder-portfolio-spine.md">ADR-006</a>
      records the decision to make the ladder the spine. The
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/docs/specs/autonomy-ladder.md">living roadmap</a>
      holds the per-level detail. A reader can check what shipped against what
      I promised.
    </p>

  </main>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
